<p>Business Activity Index at 53.8%; New Orders Index at 55.9%; Employment Index at 51.4%; Supplier Deliveries Index at 56.7%</p><p><span class="legendSpanClass">TEMPE, Ariz.</span>, <span class="legendSpanClass">Nov. 4, 2026</span> /PRNewswire/ -- Economic activity in the <b>services sector continued to expand in October</b>, say the nation's purchasing and supply executives in the latest <b>ISM</b><b><sup>®</sup></b><b><i>&nbsp;Services PMI</i></b><b><i><sup>®</sup></i></b><b><i> Report</i></b><b>. </b>The Services PMI<sup>®</sup>&nbsp;registered 54.5 percent, the 28th consecutive month in expansion territory.</p>
    
<p>The report was issued today by Steve Miller, CPSM, CSCP, Chair of the Institute for Supply Management<sup>® </sup>(ISM<sup>®</sup>) Services Business Survey Committee: "In October, the Services PMI<sup>®</sup>&nbsp;registered 54.5 percent, a decrease of 0.4 percentage point compared to September's figure of 54.9 percent. The Business Activity Index remained in expansion territory in October, decreasing 2.7 percentage points to 53.8 percent from September's reading of 56.5 percent. The New Orders Index registered 55.9 percent, 3.9 percentage points below September's figure of 59.8 percent. The Employment Index remained in expansion territory for the second month in a row with a reading of 51.4 percent, a 1.3-percentage point increase from the 50.1 percent recorded in September.</p><p>"The Supplier Deliveries Index registered 56.7 percent, 3.5 percentage points higher than the 53.2 percent recorded in September. This is the 23rd consecutive month that the index has been in expansion territory, indicating slower supplier delivery performance. (Supplier Deliveries is the only ISM<sup>®</sup>&nbsp;PMI<sup>®</sup>&nbsp;Reports index that is inversed; a reading of above 50 percent indicates slower deliveries, which is typical as the economy improves and customer demand increases.)</p><p>"The Prices Index registered above 70 percent for the seventh time in eight months; the reading of 73 percent in October is 1 percentage point below September's figure of 74 percent. The index has exceeded 60 percent for 23 straight months, and its 12-month average increased by 0.2 percentage point to 69.2 percent, the highest since April 2023.</p><p>"The Inventories Index registered 55.4 percent, down 2.4 percentage points from September's figure of 57.8 percent. The Inventory Sentiment Index expanded for the 42nd consecutive month, registering 52.1 percent, up 0.4 percentage point from September's figure of 51.7 percent. The Backlog of Orders Index recorded a ninth straight month in expansion territory, increasing 1.4 percentage points to 58 percent in October from September's reading of 56.6 percent. The New Export Orders returned to expansion territory after one month in contraction, registering 55.2 percent, an increase of 8.3 percentage points from the 46.9 percent recorded in September. The Imports Index recorded a fourth straight month in expansion territory; the figure of 54.2 percent in October is an increase of 1.3 percentage points compared to the 52.9 percent registered in September.</p><p>"Fifteen industries indicated growth in October, two more than the previous month, while two reported contraction, two fewer than in September. The October Services PMI<sup>®</sup>&nbsp;reading of 54.5 percent is 0.2 percentage point above the 12-month average of 54.3 percent. The uptick of 0.2 percentage point over September's 12-month average of 54.1 percent marks the 10th straight month that figure has increased."</p><p>Miller continues, "No commodities were reported as down in price in October, with fuel reported as up in price for a ninth month in a row. Memory products were reported as up in price for the 10th month in a row, and memory components were reported as in short supply. The number of commodities in short supply decreased from seven to three, all related to computer technologies. The Supplier Deliveries Index continued to indicate slower performance, increasing for a second month in a row with an increase to 56.7 percent, 2.5 percentage points above its 12-month average of 54.2 percent.</p><p>"The Employment Index (51.4 percent) expanded for its second month in a row and is 2.1 percentage points above its 12-month average of 49.3 percent. The New Orders, Business Activity, and Inventory Sentiment indexes are below their 12-month averages, but for the first time since February 2026, all 10 indexes were in expansion territory.</p><p>"Tariffs and fuel cost impacts continued to be the most-cited issues impacting respondents' supply chains, while many respondents indicated they are not yet seeing the full impact of these cost escalations or have not passed them through to customers. This indicates that more pricing pressure is on the way if oil and tariff cost impacts are not mitigated, even if they do not increase from current levels. Commentary on supply chain constraints increased significantly month over month, while the Backlog of Orders Index increased to 58 percent, its highest reading since it registered 58.3 percent in July 2022. The commodities identified as in short supply, however, decreased month over month, from seven to three. The Employment Index's second consecutive reading above 50 percent may indicate help is on the way for slowing supplier deliveries and increasing order backlogs. While the Backlog of Orders index hit its highest level since July 2022, the Supplier Deliveries Index reading in October is the second highest in that timeframe, exceeded only by April 2026 (56.8 percent)."</p><p><b>INDUSTRY PERFORMANCE<br /></b>The 15 services industries reporting growth in October — listed in order — are:&nbsp;Transportation &amp; Warehousing; Wholesale Trade; Information; Retail Trade; Arts, Entertainment &amp; Recreation; Finance &amp; Insurance; Other Services; Real Estate, Rental &amp; Leasing; Accommodation &amp; Food Services; Agriculture, Forestry, Fishing &amp; Hunting; Utilities; Educational Services; Management of Companies &amp; Support Services; Professional, Scientific &amp; Technical Services; and Public Administration. The two industries reporting a contraction in the month of October are: Construction; and Health Care &amp; Social Assistance.</p><p><b>WHAT RESPONDENTS ARE SAYING</b></p><ul><li>"Fuel and freight markets continue to exert pressure on distribution cost planning. The continued war in Ukraine and drought in the Midwest along with fuel and fertilizer costs are driving up the cost of wheat (30 percent year-over-year)." [Accommodation &amp; Food Services]</li><li>"Overall demand is sluggish." [Agriculture, Forestry, Fishing &amp; Hunting]</li><li>"Activity remains steady, with a strong backlog of institutional and commercial projects. Material availability has generally improved, though some mechanical and electrical equipment still requires early procurement. Labor shortages and wage pressure continue to impact the market, while higher financing costs are causing owners to closely evaluate project budgets and schedules. Overall outlook remains cautiously positive." [Construction]</li><li>"Purchasing operations continue to be impacted by elevated costs for goods and services, particularly in construction, facilities maintenance, technology and contracted services. Ongoing uncertainty surrounding tariffs, labor availability and broader economic conditions continues to influence supplier pricing and procurement planning. While overall supply chain conditions have stabilized compared to previous years, suppliers remain cautious with pricing commitments, and cost pressures continue to affect budgets." [Educational Services]</li><li>"Our company's AI utilization is increasing, but we have guardrails in place to cap <span>token</span> utilization and evaluate TCO. The price of diesel has impacted our commercial trucking business, and Hurricane Isaias temporarily caused a moratorium on binding certain policies in parts of Alabama, Mississippi and Florida." [Finance &amp; Insurance]</li><li>"Fuel prices are impacting many of our consumables and delivered goods. This has reduced margins, as we have not passed the expense to our customers because we believe it will be a short-term condition." [Information]</li><li>"While business activity remains strong, the impacts of tariffs, increased fuel prices, and longer lead times are becoming more challenging." [Management of Companies &amp; Support Services]</li><li>"Tariffs are causing havoc with international merchandise sourcing." [Retail Trade]</li><li>"Generation demand is strong. Supplies for services, equipment and materials are constrained. Nuclear uprates (or power level increases) and next-generation plants have strong tailwinds but regulatory headwinds." [Utilities]</li><li>"Tariff policy remains the main driver of landed cost: Section 301 duties in force since late July replaced the expired flat tariff, raising duties on Southeast Asian canned and dry goods. Ocean freight from Asia remains elevated and volatile, and U.S. East Coast port congestion is expected in November. El Niño is constraining fish supply, and we have suspended one source. Some Asian suppliers have delayed shipments by weeks or months and are raising prices on beverage items and reducing prices on others. Inventory is high in aggregate but unevenly distributed, so service gaps persist on selected items." [Wholesale Trade]</li></ul><div><div class="divOverflow"><div class="table-responsive"><table><tr><td class="prngen2" colspan="10" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span"><b>ISM</b><b><sup>®</sup></b><b>&nbsp;SERVICES SURVEY </b><b>RESULTS AT A GLANCE</b></span></p><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span"><b>COMPARISON OF ISM</b><b><sup>®</sup></b><b> SERVICES AND ISM</b><b><sup>®</sup></b><b> MANUFACTURING SURVEYS</b></span></p><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span"><b>OCTOBER 2026</b></span></p></td></tr><tr><td class="prngen3" colspan="1" rowspan="2"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">Index</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="6" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span"><b>Services PMI</b><b><sup>®</sup></b></span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="3" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span"><b>Manufacturing PMI</b><b><sup>®</sup></b></span></p></td></tr><tr><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">Series<br />Index</span></p><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">Oct</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4