rritory at 55.9 percent in October, 3.9 percentage points lower than the reading of 59.8 percent in September. The index has expanded for 17 consecutive months. Comments from respondents include:&nbsp;"New orders are growing at a moderate, slower pace; production remains stable with no major surge" and "Excess speculative home inventory must sell before we can start new ones."</p><p>The 15 industries reporting an increase in new orders for the month of October — listed in order — are:&nbsp;Transportation &amp; Warehousing; Other Services; Arts, Entertainment &amp; Recreation; Wholesale Trade; Finance &amp; Insurance; Information; Agriculture, Forestry, Fishing &amp; Hunting; Real Estate, Rental &amp; Leasing; Retail Trade; Management of Companies &amp; Support Services; Public Administration; Professional, Scientific &amp; Technical Services; Health Care &amp; Social Assistance; Educational Services; and Utilities. For the third month in a row, the only industry reporting a decrease in new orders in the month of October is Construction.</p><div><div class="divOverflow"><div class="table-responsive"><table><tr><td class="prngen9" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">New Orders</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">%Higher</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">%Same</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">%Lower</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">Index</span></p></td></tr><tr><td class="prngen9" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">Oct 2026</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">26.1</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">64.