terials and services increased in October for the 113th consecutive month. The Prices Index registered 73 percent, a decrease of 1 percentage point from September's reading of 74 percent. October's reading is 3.8 percentage points above its 12-month average of 69.2.</p><p>Seventeen industries reported an increase in prices paid during the month of October, in the following order:&nbsp;Other Services; Transportation &amp; Warehousing; Construction; Arts, Entertainment &amp; Recreation; Professional, Scientific &amp; Technical Services; Information; Public Administration; Management of Companies &amp; Support Services; Finance &amp; Insurance; Utilities; Accommodation &amp; Food Services; Mining; Retail Trade; Educational Services; Health Care &amp; Social Assistance; Wholesale Trade; and Real Estate, Rental &amp; Leasing. No industries reported a decrease in prices paid.</p><div><div class="divOverflow"><div class="table-responsive"><table><tr><td class="prngen9" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">Prices</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">%Higher</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">%Same</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">%Lower</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">Index</span></p></td></tr><tr><td class="prngen9" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">Oct 2026</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">48.6</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">47.4</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">4.0</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">73.0</span></p></td></tr><tr><td class="prngen9" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">Sep 2026</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">50.3</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">47.5</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">2.2</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">74.0</span></p></td></tr><tr><td class="prngen9" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">Aug 2026</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">44.8</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">52.9</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">2.3</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">72.6</span></p></td></tr><tr><td class="prngen9" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">Jul 2026</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">44.7</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">52.1</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">3.2</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">70.3</span></p></td></tr></table></div></div></div><p>NOTE: Commodities reported as up in price and down in price are listed in the commodities section of this report.</p><p><u><b>Backlog of Orders<br /></b></u>The ISM<sup>®</sup>&nbsp;Services Backlog of Orders Index registered 58 percent, a 1.4-percentage point increase compared to the 56.6 percent reported in September and the highest since July 2022 (58.3 percent). The index has been in expansion territory for nine straight months, its longest continuous growth since a string of 26 months that ended in February 2023. Of the total respondents in October, 33 percent indicated they do not measure backlog of orders. Respondent comments include: "Due to the need to hire additional staff to meet demand, we must backlog patients until a provider is available" and "Supplier capacity constraints and delayed deliveries are contributing to execution backlog on certain projects."</p><p>The 11 industries reporting an increase in order backlogs in October — in the following order — are: Finance &amp; Insurance; Arts, Entertainment &amp; Recreation; Real Estate, Rental &amp; Leasing; Retail Trade; Agriculture, Forestry, Fishing &amp; Hunting; Management of Companies &amp; Support Services; Mining; Professional, Scientific &amp; Technical Services; Wholesale Trade; Health Care &amp; Social Assistance; and Utilities. The three industries reporting a decrease in order backlogs in October are: Transportation &amp; Warehousing; Construction; and Other Services.</p><div><div class="divOverflow"><div class="table-responsive"><table><tr><td class="prngen9" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">Backlog of <br />Orders</span></p></td><td class="prngen10" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">%Higher</span></p></td><td class="prngen10" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">%Same</span></p></td><td class="prngen10" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">%Lower</span></p></td><td class="prngen10" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">Index</span></p></td></tr><tr><td class="prngen9" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">Oct 2026</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" 