/p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">52.0</span></p></td></tr></table></div></div></div><p><u><b>Imports<br /></b></u>The Imports Index was in expansion territory in October for the fourth consecutive month and the seventh time in eight months, registering 54.2 percent, 1.3 percentage points higher than the 52.9 percent reported in September. Of the total respondents in October, 38 percent reported that they do not use, or do not track the use of, imported materials.&nbsp;Respondent comments include: "Longer time for ocean freight plus higher energy costs are resulting in higher overall cost for ocean freight" and "We are actively trying to source more products from U.S.-Mexico-Canada Agreement suppliers to mitigate the steep tariffs on food, apparel, and electronics from Asia."</p><p>The eight industries reporting an increase in imports for the month of October, in order, are:&nbsp;Mining; Public Administration; Retail Trade; Professional, Scientific &amp; Technical Services; Health Care &amp; Social Assistance; Transportation &amp; Warehousing; Wholesale Trade; and Utilities. The two industries reporting a decrease in imports in October are: Accommodation &amp; Food Services; and Information. Eight industries reported no change in imports in October. Eight industries reported no change in imports in October.</p><div><div class="divOverflow"><div class="table-responsive"><table><tr><td class="prngen9" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">Imports</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">%Higher</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">%Same</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">%Lower</span></p></td><td class="prngen2" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="prnml4"><span class="prnews_span">Index<